  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 137 AM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nI'm not sure if I have anything insightful to add in this forecast\ndiscussion, as the forecast pattern over the next several days has\nnot changed significantly in quite a while.\n\nWeak south-southwesterly flow aloft is noted across the forecast\nguidance today. A modest plume of moisture is still evident on\nwater vapor satellite across portions of southeastern Colorado,\nwhich should nudge its way into our forecast area later today.\nThat should lead to a minor increase in surface moisture this\nafternoon, with surface dew points in the upper 30s to low 40s\nacross the metro. Forecast soundings aren't impressive, but do\nfeature meager CAPE (~100-250 J/kg) and sufficient DCAPE for a few\ngusty showers or perhaps a weak thunderstorm later this afternoon.\nFurther east, where >50F Td exists, a few thunderstorms could\ndevelop along the instability gradient - generally to the\neast of a Fort Morgan to Limon line. The HRRR is rather bullish on\nthe potential across far northeast Colorado, though most other\nCAMs don't show nearly as much convective coverage. Meanwhile, it\nwill still be hot today, with highs again in the low to mid 90s.\n\nContinued gradual moisture advection is expected on Sunday as we\nremain on the western periphery of a large ridge centered over\nwestern Oklahoma. While there will be a little more instability to\nwork with (MLCAPE >500 J/kg), there is essentially no synoptic\nsupport and any afternoon convection would be initially be\nconfined to the higher elevations... and any storms across the\nI-25 corridor or plains would be dependent on boundary\ninteractions. Temperatures should be slightly cooler but forecast\nhighs are still around 90F for the plains.\n\nMoisture will continue to slowly increase for early next week. We\nmay briefly have some upper level support from the right entrance\nof a 70-80kt jet streak moving across northwestern Colorado Monday\nevening/night, which will lead to increased chances of\nprecipitation across the region.\n\nThe forecast pattern becomes a little less clear by Tuesday, as\nthe ridge begins to flatten/stretch west westward. Either very\nweak flow aloft or zonal flow is expected to develop over the\nforecast area by Tuesday or Wednesday, though a decent amount of\nmoisture remains trapped under the ridge here. A look at ensemble\nmembers overnight still implies high confidence in above normal\ntemperatures continuing in this period, with lower confidence in\nhow much moisture/precipitation to expect across the region.",
